The attached image (Figure 3) illustrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response at the injection site and is taken up by recruited immune cells. Panel A, a line graph, shows the temporal dynamics of various inflammatory cells, highlighting an early peak in neutrophils (6h) followed by an increase in Mac-2+ macrophages, antigen-presenting cells, and Ly6C+ monocytes that peak around Day 1-2 and then decline by Day 7. Panel B presents confocal microscopy images demonstrating the intracellular presence of Nano-11 (red) within MHCII+, Ly6C+, and Mac-2+ cells at the injection site, confirming its uptake by these immune cells over the first few days post-injection.

Nano-11, as a nanoparticle adjuvant, could significantly contribute to cancer research by enhancing the efficacy of therapeutic cancer vaccines. Its ability to efficiently deliver antigens to antigen-presenting cells (APCs), particularly migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs), and induce their activation, including increased expression of costimulatory molecules and IL-1β secretion, is crucial for priming robust anti-tumor immune responses. The transient and localized inflammatory response, coupled with a lack of systemic distribution, suggests a favorable safety profile, making it an attractive platform for developing safer and more effective cancer immunotherapies.